    -- Metadata for a single firewall engine rule. Use it to disable a rule without removing it and to attach a description. When you describe a rule group in RULES_STRING format, Network Firewall renders a disabled rule and its description as # comment lines above the rule.
    record RuleMetadata
        -- Specifies whether the rule is active. The default is TRUE. When you set this to FALSE, Network Firewall keeps the rule in the rule group but doesn't evaluate it against traffic and doesn't count it toward the rule group's consumed capacity.
        RuleEnabled: boolean
        -- A description of the rule, up to 512 characters. The description can span multiple lines. A description line can't itself look like a firewall rule; to disable a rule, set RuleEnabled to FALSE instead.
        RuleDescription: string
    end

    -- Additional settings for a stateful rule. This is part of the StatefulRule configuration.
    record RuleOption
        -- The keyword for the Suricata compatible rule option. You must include a sid (signature ID), and can optionally include other keywords. For information about Suricata compatible keywords, see Rule options in the Suricata documentation.
        Keyword: string
        -- The settings of the Suricata compatible rule option. Rule options have zero or more setting values, and the number of possible and required settings depends on the Keyword. For more information about the settings for specific options, see Rule options.
        Settings: {string}
    end

    -- A single structured firewall engine rule, for use in a firewall engine rule group or as an embedded rule in a Next Generation policy. A firewall rule accepts, drops, or rejects traffic at a connection-state hook. For more information about authoring firewall engine rules, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
    record FirewallRule
        -- The action that the firewall engine takes on traffic that matches the rule. The firewall engine supports the following actions:
        -- ACCEPT - Allow the traffic.
        -- DROP - Block the traffic and discontinue inspection.
        -- REJECT - Block the traffic and send a TCP reset packet back to the client.
        -- REJECT_DST - Block the traffic and send a TCP reset packet to the destination.
        -- REJECT_BOTH - Block the traffic and send a TCP reset packet to both the client and the destination.
        -- You combine the action with an action scope, for example accept:flow. To send accepted traffic straight to its destination and skip the IPS engine, pair an accept action with the BypassIPSEnabled setting. For more information, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        Action: string
        -- The scope that controls how much of the matched traffic the action applies to. The valid scopes depend on the action: ACCEPT supports all four scopes, and DROP, REJECT, REJECT_DST, and REJECT_BOTH support only PACKET and FLOW.
        -- PACKET - The current packet only.
        -- HOOK - The current hook only. The firewall engine evaluates the connection again at the next hook.
        -- TX - The current transaction. The firewall engine continues to evaluate later transactions on the connection.
        -- FLOW - The entire connection. For an accept action, the connection skips the firewall engine's remaining hooks and advances to the IPS engine.
        -- In a Suricata-compatible rule string, you write the scope in lowercase, for example accept:flow.
        ActionScope: string
        -- The connection-state hook at which the firewall engine evaluates the rule. The hook encodes both the protocol and the connection state, for example a TCP hook or a TLS client_hello hook. For the hooks that the firewall engine supports and how they map to the connection lifecycle, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide and the Suricata firewall mode documentation.
        Hook: string
        -- The protocol of the rule's hook. The protocol is derived from the hook and must match the hook's protocol prefix. Network Firewall rejects a rule whose protocol doesn't match its hook.
        Protocol: string
        -- The source IP addresses and address ranges to inspect for, in CIDR notation. A firewall rule always compiles to a forward-direction rule, so you express inbound compared to outbound traffic through the source and destination addresses.
        SourceIPs: string
        -- The destination IP addresses and address ranges to inspect for, in CIDR notation. A firewall rule always compiles to a forward-direction rule, so you express inbound compared to outbound traffic through the source and destination addresses.
        DestIPs: string
        -- The source ports to inspect for.
        SourcePorts: string
        -- The destination ports to inspect for.
        DestPorts: string
        -- Additional options for the rule. These are the Suricata RuleOptions settings, such as the signature ID (sid) and match keywords.
        RuleOptions: {RuleOption}
        -- Whether Network Firewall automatically accepts the earlier application-layer hooks that a connection must pass to reach this rule's hook. Because the firewall engine is drop by default, accepting prior hooks lets you author a single rule for the hook you care about, instead of also authoring a rule for each earlier hook. This setting accepts the earlier application-layer hooks only; it doesn't accept the TCP layer. For more information, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        AcceptPriorHooks: boolean
        -- Specifies whether the rule also generates an alert log for the traffic that it matches. Turning on alerting adds a secondary alert action to the rule, so that Network Firewall logs the match in addition to taking the rule's primary action.
        AlertEnabled: boolean
        -- Specifies whether an accepted connection bypasses the IPS engine. By default, traffic that the firewall engine accepts advances to the IPS engine for threat inspection. When you turn on this setting for an accept rule, the accepted traffic goes straight to its destination without IPS inspection. You can set the bypass only on accept rules, and it uses the same scope as the rule's accept action; only the flow and packet scopes support a bypass. For more information, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        BypassIPSEnabled: boolean
        -- Optional metadata for the rule: whether the rule is enabled and a description. Metadata doesn't change how the rule matches or acts on traffic.
        RuleMetadata: RuleMetadata
    end

    enum GeneratedRulesType
        "ALLOWLIST"
        "DENYLIST"
        "REJECTLIST"
        "ALERTLIST"
    end

    enum TargetType
        "TLS_SNI"
        "HTTP_HOST"
        "AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN"
    end

    -- Stateful inspection criteria for a domain list rule group.
    -- For HTTPS traffic, domain filtering is SNI-based. It uses the server name indicator extension of the TLS handshake.
    -- By default, Network Firewall domain list inspection only includes traffic coming from the VPC where you deploy the firewall. To inspect traffic from IP addresses outside of the deployment VPC, you set the HOME_NET rule variable to include the CIDR range of the deployment VPC plus the other CIDR ranges. For more information, see RuleVariables in this guide and
    -- Stateful domain list rule groups in Network Firewall in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
    record RulesSourceList
        -- The domains that you want to inspect for in your traffic flows. Valid domain specifications are the following:
        -- Explicit names. For example, abc.example.com matches only the domain abc.example.com.
        -- Names that use a domain wildcard, which you indicate with an initial '.'. For example,.example.com matches example.com and matches all subdomains of example.com, such as abc.example.com and www.example.com.
        Targets: {string}
        -- The types of matching to perform against the domains in Targets. You can specify any combination of the following:
        -- AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN – Match the domain against the domain that the Amazon Route 53 Resolver resolved the connection's destination IP address for.
        -- HTTP_HOST – Match the domain against the Host header of HTTP traffic.
        -- TLS_SNI – Match the domain against the Server Name Indication (SNI) of HTTPS traffic.
        -- AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN matches on how the destination IP address was resolved, rather than on the domain that the client asserts in its TLS SNI or HTTP Host header. This target type takes effect when you configure resolved IP validation on the firewall policy. For more information, see the Route53ResolvedIPValidation firewall policy setting.
        -- Whether you include AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN changes what the generated rules match, together with GeneratedRulesType:
        -- In an ALLOWLIST, combining AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN with TLS_SNI or HTTP_HOST requires both conditions to match: the SNI or Host header matches the domain, and the Route 53 Resolver resolved the destination IP address for that domain. Network Firewall allows the traffic only when both conditions match, so it blocks traffic that matches just one—for example, a spoofed Host header sent to an unresolved IP address.
        -- In a DENYLIST, or when AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN is the only target type, Network Firewall evaluates each condition independently: a match on either the domain (SNI or Host) or the resolved destination IP address triggers the rule.
        -- When you don't include AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN, the generated rules match only on the SNI or Host header, without checking how the destination IP address was resolved.
        -- An ALLOWLIST whose only target type is AWS_ROUTE_53_RESOLVED_DOMAIN is additive: it allows matching traffic but doesn't generate a default rule that drops non-matching traffic. Only the TLS_SNI and HTTP_HOST target types generate that default drop. To allow only the resolved domains and drop all other traffic, include a protocol target type as well.
        TargetTypes: {string}
        -- Whether you want to apply allow, reject, alert, or drop behavior to the domains in your target list.
        -- When logging is enabled and you choose Alert, traffic that matches the domain specifications
        -- generates an alert in the firewall's logs. Then, traffic either passes, is rejected, or drops based on other rules in the firewall policy.
        GeneratedRulesType: string
    end

    -- The source of the rules for a firewall engine rule group. You provide the rules as a single Suricata-compatible rule string, as a list of structured firewall rules, or as a list of domain names to generate rules from.
    record FirewallRulesSource
        -- Firewall engine rules, provided as a single Suricata-compatible rule string in firewall mode. Use this to provide rules that you've already written in the Suricata firewall-mode format. For the rule format, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        RulesString: string
        -- An array of individual firewall engine rules, provided as structured rules rather than as a rule string.
        FirewallRules: {FirewallRule}
        -- Stateful inspection criteria for a domain list, specified as the list of domain names that Network Firewall generates firewall engine rules from.
        RulesSourceList: RulesSourceList
    end

    -- A set of port ranges for use in the rules in a rule group.
    record PortSet
        -- The set of port ranges.
        Definition: {string}
    end

    -- Settings that are available for use in the rules in the RuleGroup
    -- where this is defined. See CreateRuleGroup or UpdateRuleGroup for usage.
    record RuleVariables
        -- A list of IP addresses and address ranges, in CIDR notation.
        IPSets: {string : IPSet}
        -- A list of port ranges.
        PortSets: {string : PortSet}
    end

    -- The object that defines a firewall engine rule group, for use in a Next Generation policy. A firewall engine rule group provides access-control rules that the firewall engine evaluates at connection-state hooks. You provide the rules as a Suricata-compatible rule string, as structured firewall rules, or generated from a list of domain names. For more information, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
    record FirewallRuleGroup
        -- The stateful inspection criteria for the firewall engine rule group.
        RulesSource: FirewallRulesSource
        -- Settings that are available for use in the rules in the firewall engine rule group, such as IP set and port variables that the rules reference.
        RuleVariables: RuleVariables
        -- The list of a rule group's reference sets. Firewall engine rule groups don't support IP set references (reference sets); this setting applies to IPS engine rule groups. To use CIDR ranges in firewall engine rules, use rule variables instead.
        ReferenceSets: ReferenceSets
    end

    -- A single entry in the firewall section of a Next Generation policy. An entry either references an existing firewall engine rule group by ARN or embeds a rule group directly in the policy, and it carries the priority that sets the rule group's evaluation order within the firewall section.
    record FirewallRuleGroupEntry
        -- The priority of the rule group entry within the firewall section. Network Firewall evaluates the firewall section's entries in order of increasing priority value. Each entry in the firewall section must have a unique priority.
        Priority: number
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an existing firewall engine rule group to reference in this entry. Provide either ResourceArn to reference an existing rule group or InlineRuleGroup to embed a rule group in the policy.
        ResourceArn: string
        -- A firewall engine rule group defined directly in the policy, instead of referenced by ARN. Provide either InlineRuleGroup to embed a rule group or ResourceArn to reference an existing rule group. An embedded rule group can't use a domain list rules source; to filter on a domain list, reference a standalone rule group by ARN instead.
        InlineRuleGroup: FirewallRuleGroup
    end

    -- The source of the rules for an IPS engine rule group.
    record IpsRulesSource
        -- IPS engine rules, provided as a single Suricata-compatible rule string. For the rule format, see Rules Format on the Suricata website.
        RulesString: string
    end

    -- The object that defines an IPS engine rule group, for use in a Next Generation policy. An IPS engine rule group provides threat-detection rules that the IPS engine evaluates in the context of the traffic flow. You provide the rules as a Suricata-compatible rule string. For more information, see Rule groups for a firewall using a Next Generation policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
    record IpsRuleGroup
        -- The stateful inspection criteria for the IPS engine rule group.
        RulesSource: IpsRulesSource
        -- Settings that are available for use in the rules in the IPS engine rule group.
        RuleVariables: RuleVariables
        -- The list of a rule group's reference sets.
        ReferenceSets: ReferenceSets
    end

    -- The setting that allows the policy owner to change the behavior of an IPS engine rule group within a Next Generation policy.
    record IpsRuleGroupOverride
        -- The action that changes the rule group from DROP to ALERT. This only applies to managed rule groups.
        Action: string
    end

    -- A single entry in the IPS section of a Next Generation policy. An entry either references an existing IPS engine rule group by ARN or embeds a rule group directly in the policy. IPS section entries don't use a priority.
    record IpsRuleGroupEntry
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of an existing IPS engine rule group to reference in this entry. Provide either ResourceArn to reference an existing rule group or InlineRuleGroup to embed a rule group in the policy.
        ResourceArn: string
        -- An IPS engine rule group defined directly in the policy, instead of referenced by ARN. Provide either InlineRuleGroup to embed a rule group or ResourceArn to reference an existing rule group.
        InlineRuleGroup: IpsRuleGroup
        -- The setting that allows the policy owner to change the behavior of the IPS engine rule group within this policy, such as changing a managed rule group's action from DROP to ALERT.
        Override: IpsRuleGroupOverride
    end

    enum PolicyMode
        "ENFORCE"
        "MONITOR"
    end

    enum StreamExceptionPolicyV2
        "DROP"
        "REJECT"
    end

    -- Configuration settings for the handling of stateful traffic in a Next Generation policy.
    record StatefulEngineOptionsV2
        -- How Network Firewall handles traffic on a network connection that breaks midstream. A Next Generation policy supports DROP and REJECT. For more information, see Stream exception policy options in your firewall policy in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        StreamExceptionPolicy: string
        -- The amount of time that can pass without any traffic sent through the firewall before the firewall determines that the connection is idle.
        FlowTimeouts: FlowTimeouts
    end

    -- The object that defines a Next Generation policy. This, along with the FirewallPolicyV2Response, define the policy.
    -- A Next Generation policy defines how the firewall engine and IPS engine inspect and control your VPC network traffic. You define the policy as a firewall section and an IPS section, each a collection of rule groups, along with policy-wide settings such as the policy mode and default actions. For more information, see Next Generation policies in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
    record FirewallPolicyV2
        -- The firewall section of the policy—the rule groups that the firewall engine evaluates. Each entry references an existing firewall engine rule group or embeds a rule group directly in the policy, and each entry has a unique priority that sets its evaluation order within the section.
        FirewallRuleGroupEntries: {FirewallRuleGroupEntry}
        -- The IPS section of the policy—the rule groups that the IPS engine evaluates. Each entry references an existing IPS engine rule group or embeds a rule group directly in the policy. IPS section entries don't use a priority.
        IpsRuleGroupEntries: {IpsRuleGroupEntry}
        -- Indicates whether the policy's engines enforce their verdicts or only report them. In ENFORCE mode, the firewall engine and IPS engine apply their verdicts to traffic. In MONITOR mode, the engines evaluate traffic and generate logs and metrics, but don't block traffic, so you can observe how a policy would behave before you enforce it. For more information, see Next Generation policies in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        PolicyMode: string
        -- The action that the firewall engine takes on traffic that doesn't match any firewall engine rule at a hook. The firewall engine is drop by default. You can set the default action to one of the following:
        -- DROP - Block the traffic.
        -- ACCEPT - Allow the traffic.
        -- REJECT - Block the traffic and send a TCP reset packet back to the client.
        -- ALERT - Allow the traffic and generate an alert log for the traffic that reaches the default action at every hook. This is distinct from setting the policy mode to MONITOR.
        -- For more information, see Next Generation policies in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        FirewallDefaultAction: string
        -- Additional options governing how the engines handle stateful traffic, such as the stream exception policy and flow timeouts.
        StatefulEngineOptions: StatefulEngineOptionsV2
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the TLS inspection configuration to use to decrypt traffic for inspection. Next Generation policies support session holding only. For more information, see Inspecting SSL/TLS traffic with TLS inspection configurations in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        TLSInspectionConfigurationArn: string
        -- Contains variables that you can use to override default Suricata settings in your Next Generation policy.
        PolicyVariables: PolicyVariables
        -- The settings that enable resolved IP validation for the Next Generation policy.
        -- Resolved IP validation verifies that a connection's destination IP address is one that the
        -- Amazon Route 53 Resolver returned in a DNS response for a domain. A VPC that's associated with
        -- the Route 53 Resolver DNS Firewall rule group that you specify must send the response.
        -- This checks how the destination IP address was obtained, instead of relying only on the
        -- domain that the client asserts in its TLS Server Name Indication (SNI) or HTTP
        -- Host header. For more information, see
        -- Route53ResolvedIPValidation.
        Route53ResolvedIPValidation: Route53ResolvedIPValidation
    end

    record CreateFirewallPolicyV2Input
        -- The descriptive name of the Next Generation policy. You can't change the name of a firewall policy after you create it.
        FirewallPolicyV2Name: string
        -- The rule groups and other settings that define the Next Generation policy.
        FirewallPolicyV2: FirewallPolicyV2
        -- A description of the firewall policy.
        Description: string
        -- The key:value pairs to associate with the resource.
        Tags: {Tag}
        -- Indicates whether you want Network Firewall to just check the validity of the request, rather than run the request.
        -- If set to TRUE, Network Firewall checks whether the request can run successfully,
        -- but doesn't actually make the requested changes. The call returns the value that the request would return if you ran it with
        -- dry run set to FALSE, but doesn't make additions or changes to your resources. This option allows you to make sure that you have
        -- the required permissions to run the request and that your request parameters are valid.
        -- If set to FALSE, Network Firewall makes the requested changes to your resources.
        DryRun: boolean
        -- A complex type that contains settings for encryption of your firewall policy resources.
        EncryptionConfiguration: EncryptionConfiguration
    end

    -- The high-level properties of a Next Generation policy. This includes the policy's identity, status, consumed capacity, and association count. Network Firewall returns this object in response to the Next Generation policy operations. To retrieve the policy's rule groups and settings, use the FirewallPolicyV2 object.
    record FirewallPolicyV2Response
        -- The descriptive name of the Next Generation policy. You can't change the name of a firewall policy after you create it.
        FirewallPolicyV2Name: string
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the Next Generation policy.
        FirewallPolicyV2Arn: string
        -- The unique identifier of the Next Generation policy. Network Firewall assigns this identifier when you create the policy.
        FirewallPolicyV2Id: string
        -- A description of the Next Generation policy.
        Description: string
        -- The current status of the Next Generation policy. A policy must be in the ACTIVE state before you can use it in a firewall.
        FirewallPolicyV2Status: string
        -- Additional information about the current status of the Next Generation policy.
        StatusMessage: string
        -- The key-value tag pairs that are associated with the Next Generation policy.
        Tags: {Tag}
        -- The number of firewall engine rule capacity units that the policy's firewall section currently consumes.
        ConsumedFirewallRuleCapacity: number
        -- The number of IPS engine rule capacity units that the policy's IPS section currently consumes.
        ConsumedIpsRuleCapacity: number
        -- The number of IPS domain list capacity units that the policy's IPS section currently consumes.
        ConsumedIpsDomainCapacity: number
        -- The number of firewalls that are associated with the Next Generation policy.
        NumberOfAssociations: number
        -- A complex type that contains the Amazon Web Services KMS encryption configuration settings for your Next Generation policy.
        EncryptionConfiguration: EncryptionConfiguration
        -- Any configuration errors that apply to the Next Generation policy. Network Firewall detects these errors asynchronously, after you create or update the policy, so an error can appear after the request that caused it has already succeeded. Each entry describes the problem and how to resolve it, and Network Firewall clears it automatically after you resolve the problem.
        Errors: {FirewallPolicyError}
        -- The last time that the Next Generation policy was changed.
        LastModifiedTime: number
    end

    record CreateFirewallPolicyV2Output
        -- A token used for optimistic locking. Network Firewall returns a token to your requests that access the firewall policy. The token marks the state of the policy resource at the time of the request.
        -- To make changes to the policy, you provide the token in your request. Network Firewall uses the token to ensure that the policy hasn't changed since you last retrieved it. If it has changed, the operation fails with an InvalidTokenException. If this happens, retrieve the firewall policy again to get a current copy of it with current token. Reapply your changes as needed, then try the operation again using the new token.
        UpdateToken: string
        -- The high-level properties of the Next Generation policy. This, along with the FirewallPolicyV2, define the policy. You can retrieve all objects for a Next Generation policy by calling DescribeFirewallPolicyV2.
        FirewallPolicyV2Response: FirewallPolicyV2Response
    end

    enum StatefulAction
        "PASS"
        "DROP"
        "ALERT"
        "REJECT"
    end

    enum StatefulRuleDirection
        "FORWARD"
        "ANY"
    end

    enum StatefulRuleProtocol
        "IP"
        "TCP"
        "UDP"
        "ICMP"
        "HTTP"
        "FTP"
        "TLS"
        "SMB"
        "DNS"
        "DCERPC"
        "SSH"
        "SMTP"
        "IMAP"
        "MSN"
        "KRB5"
        "IKEV2"
        "TFTP"
        "NTP"
        "DHCP"
        "HTTP2"
        "QUIC"
    end

    -- The basic rule criteria for Network Firewall to use to inspect packet headers in stateful
    -- traffic flow inspection. Traffic flows that match the criteria are a match for the
    -- corresponding StatefulRule.
    record Header
        -- The protocol to inspect for. To specify all, you can use IP, because all traffic on Amazon Web Services and on the internet is IP.
        Protocol: string
        -- The source IP address or address range to inspect for, in CIDR notation.
        -- To match with any address, specify ANY.
        -- Specify an IP address or a block of IP addresses in Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR) notation. Network Firewall supports all address ranges for IPv4 and IPv6.
        -- Examples:
        -- To configure Network Firewall to inspect for the IP address 192.0.2.44, specify 192.0.2.44/32.
        -- To configure Network Firewall to inspect for IP addresses from 192.0.2.0 to 192.0.2.255, specify 192.0.2.0/24.
        -- To configure Network Firewall to inspect for the IP address 1111:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0111, specify 1111:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0111/128.
        -- To configure Network Firewall to inspect for IP addresses from 1111:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000 to 1111:0000:0000:0000:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff, specify 1111:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000/64.
        -- For more information about CIDR notation, see the Wikipedia entry Classless
        -- Inter-Domain Routing.
        Source: string
        -- The source port to inspect for. You can specify an individual port,
        -- for example 1994 and you can specify a port range, for example 1990:1994.
        -- To match with any port, specify ANY.
        SourcePort: string
        -- The direction of traffic flow to inspect. If set to ANY, the inspection
        -- matches bidirectional traffic, both from the source to the destination and from the
        -- destination to the source. If set to FORWARD, the inspection only matches
        -- traffic going from the source to the destination.
        Direction: string
        -- The destination IP address or address range to inspect for, in CIDR notation.
        -- To match with any address, specify ANY.
        -- Specify an IP address or a block of IP addresses in Classless Inter-Domain Routing (CIDR) notation. Network Firewall supports all address ranges for IPv4 and IPv6.
        -- Examples:
        -- To configure Network Firewall to inspect for the IP address 192.0.2.44, specify 192.0.2.44/32.
        -- To configure Network Firewall to inspect for IP addresses from 192.0.2.0 to 192.0.2.255, specify 192.0.2.0/24.
        -- To configure Network Firewall to inspect for the IP address 1111:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0111, specify 1111:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0111/128.
        -- To configure Network Firewall to inspect for IP addresses from 1111:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000 to 1111:0000:0000:0000:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff, specify 1111:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000:0000/64.
        -- For more information about CIDR notation, see the Wikipedia entry Classless
        -- Inter-Domain Routing.
        Destination: string
        -- The destination port to inspect for. You can specify an individual port,
        -- for example 1994 and you can specify a port range, for example 1990:1994.
        -- To match with any port, specify ANY.
        DestinationPort: string
    end

    -- A single Suricata rules specification, for use in a stateful rule group.
    -- Use this option to specify a simple Suricata rule with protocol, source and destination, ports, direction, and rule options.
    -- For information about the Suricata Rules format, see
    -- Rules Format on the Suricata website.
    record StatefulRule
        -- Defines what Network Firewall should do with the packets in a traffic flow when the flow
        -- matches the stateful rule criteria. For all actions, Network Firewall performs the specified
        -- action and discontinues stateful inspection of the traffic flow.
        -- The actions for a stateful rule are defined as follows:
        -- PASS - Permits the packets to go to the
        -- intended destination.
        -- DROP - Blocks the packets from going to
        -- the intended destination and sends an alert log message, if alert logging is configured in the Firewall
        -- LoggingConfiguration.
        -- ALERT - Sends an alert log message, if alert logging is configured in the Firewall
        -- LoggingConfiguration.
        -- You can use this action to test a rule that you intend to use to drop traffic. You
        -- can enable the rule with ALERT action, verify in the logs that the rule
        -- is filtering as you want, then change the action to DROP.
        -- REJECT - Drops traffic that matches the conditions of the stateful rule, and sends a TCP reset packet back to sender of the packet. A TCP reset packet is a packet with no payload and an RST bit contained in the TCP header flags. REJECT is available only for TCP traffic. This option doesn't support FTP or IMAP protocols.
        Action: string
        -- The stateful inspection criteria for this rule, used to inspect traffic flows.
        Header: Header
        -- Additional options for the rule. These are the Suricata RuleOptions settings.
        RuleOptions: {RuleOption}
    end

    -- A single port range specification. This is used for source and destination port ranges
    -- in the stateless rule MatchAttributes, SourcePorts, and
    -- DestinationPorts settings.
    record PortRange
        -- The lower limit of the port range. This must be less than or equal to the
        -- ToPort specification.
        FromPort: number
        -- The upper limit of the port range. This must be greater than or equal to the
        -- FromPort specification.
        ToPort: number
    end

    enum TCPFlag
        "FIN"
        "SYN"
        "RST"
        "PSH"
        "ACK"
        "URG"
        "ECE"
        "CWR"
    end

    -- TCP flags and masks to inspect packets for, used in stateless rules MatchAttributes settings.
    record TCPFlagField
        -- Used in conjunction with the Masks setting to define the flags that must be set and flags that must not be set in order for the packet to match. This setting can only specify values that are also specified in the Masks setting.
        -- For the flags that are specified in the masks setting, the following must be true for the packet to match:
        -- The ones that are set in this flags setting must be set in the packet.
        -- The ones that are not set in this flags setting must also not be set in the packet.
        Flags: {string}
        -- The set of flags to consider in the inspection. To inspect all flags in the valid values list, leave this with no setting.
        Masks: {string}
    end

    -- Criteria for Network Firewall to use to inspect an individual packet in stateless rule inspection. Each match attributes set can include one or more items such as IP address, CIDR range, port number, protocol, and TCP flags.
    record MatchAttributes
        -- The source IP addresses and address ranges to inspect for, in CIDR notation. If not
        -- specified, this matches with any source address.
        Sources: {Address}
        -- The destination IP addresses and address ranges to inspect for, in CIDR notation. If not
        -- specified, this matches with any destination address.
        Destinations: {Address}
        -- The source port to inspect for. You can specify an individual port,
        -- for example 1994 and you can specify a port range, for example 1990:1994.
        -- To match with any port, specify ANY.
        -- If not specified, this matches with any source port.
        -- This setting is only used for protocols 6 (TCP) and 17 (UDP).
        SourcePorts: {PortRange}
        -- The destination port to inspect for. You can specify an individual port,
        -- for example 1994 and you can specify a port range, for example 1990:1994.
        -- To match with any port, specify ANY.
        -- This setting is only used for protocols 6 (TCP) and 17 (UDP).
        DestinationPorts: {PortRange}
        -- The protocols to inspect for, specified using the assigned internet protocol number (IANA)
        -- for each protocol. If not specified, this matches with any protocol.
        Protocols: {number}
        -- The TCP flags and masks to inspect for. If not specified, this matches with any
        -- settings. This setting is only used for protocol 6 (TCP).
        TCPFlags: {TCPFlagField}
    end

    -- The inspection criteria and action for a single stateless rule. Network Firewall inspects each packet for the specified matching
    -- criteria. When a packet matches the criteria, Network Firewall performs the rule's actions on
    -- the packet.
    record RuleDefinition
        -- Criteria for Network Firewall to use to inspect an individual packet in stateless rule inspection. Each match attributes set can include one or more items such as IP address, CIDR range, port number, protocol, and TCP flags.
        MatchAttributes: MatchAttributes
        -- The actions to take on a packet that matches one of the stateless rule definition's
        -- match attributes. You must specify a standard action and you can add custom actions.
        -- Network Firewall only forwards a packet for stateful rule inspection if you specify
        -- aws:forward_to_sfe for a rule that the packet matches, or if the packet
        -- doesn't match any stateless rule and you specify aws:forward_to_sfe for the
        -- StatelessDefaultActions setting for the FirewallPolicy.
        -- For every rule, you must specify exactly one of the following standard actions.
        -- aws:pass - Discontinues all inspection of
        -- the packet and permits it to go to its intended destination.
        -- aws:drop - Discontinues all inspection of
        -- the packet and blocks it from going to its intended destination.
        -- aws:forward_to_sfe - Discontinues
        -- stateless inspection of the packet and forwards it to the stateful rule engine for
        -- inspection.
        -- Additionally, you can specify a custom action.
        -- To
        -- do this, you define a custom action by name and type, then provide the name you've assigned
        -- to the action in this Actions setting. For information about the options, see
        -- CustomAction.
        -- To provide more than one action in this setting, separate the settings with a comma. For
        -- example, if you have a custom PublishMetrics action that you've named
        -- MyMetricsAction, then you could specify the standard action
        -- aws:pass and the custom action with [“aws:pass”,
        -- “MyMetricsAction”].
        Actions: {string}
    end

    -- A single stateless rule. This is used in StatelessRulesAndCustomActions.
    record StatelessRule
        -- Defines the stateless 5-tuple packet inspection criteria and the action to take on a
        -- packet that matches the criteria.
        RuleDefinition: RuleDefinition
        -- Indicates the order in which to run this rule relative to all of the
        -- rules that are defined for a stateless rule group. Network Firewall evaluates the rules in a
        -- rule group starting with the lowest priority setting. You must ensure that the priority
        -- settings are unique for the rule group.
        -- Each stateless rule group uses exactly one StatelessRulesAndCustomActions
        -- object, and each StatelessRulesAndCustomActions contains exactly one
        -- StatelessRules object. To ensure unique priority settings for your rule
        -- groups, set unique priorities for the stateless rules that you define inside any single
        -- StatelessRules object.
        -- You can change the priority settings of your rules at any time. To make it easier to
        -- insert rules later, number them so there's a wide range in between, for example use 100,
        -- 200, and so on.
        Priority: number
    end

    -- Stateless inspection criteria. Each stateless rule group uses exactly one of these data
    -- types to define its stateless rules.
    record StatelessRulesAndCustomActions
        -- Defines the set of stateless rules for use in a stateless rule group.
        StatelessRules: {StatelessRule}
        -- Defines an array of individual custom action definitions that are available for use by
        -- the stateless rules in this StatelessRulesAndCustomActions specification. You
        -- name each custom action that you define, and then you can use it by name in your StatelessRule
        -- RuleDefinition
        -- Actions specification.
        CustomActions: {CustomAction}
    end

    -- The stateless or stateful rules definitions for use in a single rule group. Each rule
    -- group requires a single RulesSource. You can use an instance of this for
    -- either stateless rules or stateful rules.
    record RulesSource
        -- Stateful inspection criteria, provided in Suricata compatible rules. Suricata is an open-source threat detection framework that includes a standard
        -- rule-based language for network traffic inspection.
        -- These rules contain the inspection criteria and the action to take for traffic that
        -- matches the criteria, so this type of rule group doesn't have a separate action
        -- setting.
        -- You can't use the priority keyword if the RuleOrder option in StatefulRuleOptions is set to STRICT_ORDER.
        RulesString: string
        -- Stateful inspection criteria for a domain list rule group.
        RulesSourceList: RulesSourceList
        -- An array of individual stateful rules inspection criteria to be used together in a stateful rule group.
        -- Use this option to specify simple Suricata rules with protocol, source and destination, ports, direction, and rule options.
        -- For information about the Suricata Rules format, see
        -- Rules Format on the Suricata website.
        StatefulRules: {StatefulRule}
        -- Stateless inspection criteria to be used in a stateless rule group.
        StatelessRulesAndCustomActions: StatelessRulesAndCustomActions
    end

    -- Additional options governing how Network Firewall handles the rule group. You can only use these for stateful rule groups.
    record StatefulRuleOptions
        -- Indicates how to manage the order of the rule evaluation for the rule group. DEFAULT_ACTION_ORDER is
        -- the default behavior. Stateful rules are provided to the rule engine as Suricata compatible strings, and Suricata evaluates them
        -- based on certain settings. For more information, see
        -- Evaluation order for stateful rules in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        RuleOrder: string
    end

    -- The object that defines the rules in a rule group. This, along with RuleGroupResponse, define the rule group. You can retrieve all objects for a rule group by calling DescribeRuleGroup.
    -- Network Firewall uses a rule group to inspect and control network traffic.
    -- You define stateless rule groups to inspect individual packets and you define stateful rule groups to inspect packets in the context of their
    -- traffic flow.
    -- To use a rule group, you include it by reference in an Network Firewall firewall policy, then you use the policy in a firewall. You can reference a rule group from
    -- more than one firewall policy, and you can use a firewall policy in more than one firewall.
    record RuleGroup
        -- Settings that are available for use in the rules in the rule group. You can only use
        -- these for stateful rule groups.
        RuleVariables: RuleVariables
        -- The list of a rule group's reference sets.
        ReferenceSets: ReferenceSets
        -- The stateful rules or stateless rules for the rule group.
        RulesSource: RulesSource
        -- Additional options governing how Network Firewall handles stateful rules. The policies where you use your stateful
        -- rule group must have stateful rule options settings that are compatible with these settings. Some limitations apply; for more information, see Strict evaluation order in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        StatefulRuleOptions: StatefulRuleOptions
    end

    -- High-level information about the managed rule group that your own rule group is copied from. You can use the the metadata to track version updates made to the originating rule group. You can retrieve all objects for a rule group by calling DescribeRuleGroup.
    record SourceMetadata
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the rule group that your own rule group is copied from.
        SourceArn: string
        -- The update token of the Amazon Web Services managed rule group that your own rule group is copied from. To determine the update token for the managed rule group, call DescribeRuleGroup.
        SourceUpdateToken: string
    end

    enum SummaryRuleOption
        "SID"
        "MSG"
        "METADATA"
    end

    -- A complex type that specifies which Suricata rule metadata fields to use when displaying threat information. Contains:
    -- RuleOptions - The Suricata rule options fields to extract and display
    -- These settings affect how threat information appears in both the console and API responses. Summaries are available for rule groups you manage and for active threat defense Amazon Web Services managed rule groups.
    record SummaryConfiguration
        -- Specifies the selected rule options returned by DescribeRuleGroupSummary.
        RuleOptions: {string}
    end

    enum RuleGroupType
        "STATELESS"
        "STATEFUL"
        "STATEFUL_DOMAIN"
        "FIREWALL"
        "IPS"
    end

    record CreateRuleGroupInput
        -- The descriptive name of the rule group. You can't change the name of a rule group after you create it.
        RuleGroupName: string
        -- An object that defines the rule group rules.
        -- You must provide either this rule group setting or a Rules setting, but not both.
        RuleGroup: RuleGroup
        -- An object that defines the rules in a firewall engine rule group, for use in a Next Generation policy.
        FirewallRuleGroup: FirewallRuleGroup
        -- An object that defines the rules in an IPS engine rule group, for use in a Next Generation policy.
        IpsRuleGroup: IpsRuleGroup
        -- A string containing stateful rule group rules specifications in Suricata flat format, with one rule
        -- per line. Use this to import your existing Suricata compatible rule groups.
        -- You must provide either this rules setting or a populated RuleGroup setting, but not both.
        -- You can provide your rule group specification in Suricata flat format through this setting when you create or update your rule group. The call
        -- response returns a RuleGroup object that Network Firewall has populated from your string.
        Rules: string
        -- Indicates the type of the rule group. Valid values are:
        -- STATELESS - Stateless rules, for use in a Classic Network Firewall policy.
        -- STATEFUL - Stateful rules, for use in a Classic Network Firewall policy.
        -- FIREWALL - Firewall engine rules, for use in a Next Generation policy.
        -- IPS - IPS engine rules, for use in a Next Generation policy.
        Type: string
        -- A description of the rule group.
        Description: string
        -- The maximum operating resources that this rule group can use. Rule group capacity is fixed at creation.
        -- When you update a rule group, you are limited to this capacity. When you reference a rule group
        -- from a firewall policy, Network Firewall reserves this capacity for the rule group.
        -- You can retrieve the capacity that would be required for a rule group before you create the rule group by calling
        -- CreateRuleGroup with DryRun set to TRUE.
        -- You can't change or exceed this capacity when you update the rule group, so leave
        -- room for your rule group to grow.
        -- Capacity for a stateless rule group
        -- For a stateless rule group, the capacity required is the sum of the capacity
        -- requirements of the individual rules that you expect to have in the rule group.
        -- To calculate the capacity requirement of a single rule, multiply the capacity
        -- requirement values of each of the rule's match settings:
        -- A match setting with no criteria specified has a value of 1.
        -- A match setting with Any specified has a value of 1.
        -- All other match settings have a value equal to the number of elements provided in
        -- the setting. For example, a protocol setting ["UDP"] and a source setting
        -- ["10.0.0.0/24"] each have a value of 1. A protocol setting ["UDP","TCP"] has a value
        -- of 2. A source setting ["10.0.0.0/24","10.0.0.1/24","10.0.0.2/24"] has a value of 3.
        -- A rule with no criteria specified in any of its match settings has a capacity
        -- requirement of 1. A rule with protocol setting ["UDP","TCP"], source setting
        -- ["10.0.0.0/24","10.0.0.1/24","10.0.0.2/24"], and a single specification or no specification
        -- for each of the other match settings has a capacity requirement of 6.
        -- Capacity for a stateful rule group
        -- For a stateful rule group, the minimum capacity required is the number of individual rules that
        -- you expect to have in the rule group.
        Capacity: number
        -- The key:value pairs to associate with the resource.
        Tags: {Tag}
        -- Indicates whether you want Network Firewall to just check the validity of the request, rather than run the request.
        -- If set to TRUE, Network Firewall checks whether the request can run successfully,
        -- but doesn't actually make the requested changes. The call returns the value that the request would return if you ran it with
        -- dry run set to FALSE, but doesn't make additions or changes to your resources. This option allows you to make sure that you have
        -- the required permissions to run the request and that your request parameters are valid.
        -- If set to FALSE, Network Firewall makes the requested changes to your resources.
        DryRun: boolean
        -- A complex type that contains settings for encryption of your rule group resources.
        EncryptionConfiguration: EncryptionConfiguration
        -- A complex type that contains metadata about the rule group that your own rule group is copied from. You can use the metadata to keep track of updates made to the originating rule group.
        SourceMetadata: SourceMetadata
        -- Indicates whether you want Network Firewall to analyze the stateless rules in the rule group for rule behavior such as asymmetric routing. If set to TRUE, Network Firewall runs the analysis and then creates the rule group for you. To run the stateless rule group analyzer without creating the rule group, set DryRun to TRUE.
        AnalyzeRuleGroup: boolean
        -- An object that contains a RuleOptions array of strings.
        -- You use RuleOptions to determine which of the following RuleSummary values are returned in response to DescribeRuleGroupSummary.
        -- Metadata - returns
        -- Msg
        -- SID
        SummaryConfiguration: SummaryConfiguration
    end

    -- The high-level properties of a rule group. This, along with the RuleGroup, define the rule group. You can retrieve all objects for a rule group by calling DescribeRuleGroup.
    record RuleGroupResponse
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the rule group.
        -- If this response is for a create request that had DryRun set to
        -- TRUE, then this ARN is a placeholder that isn't attached to a valid
        -- resource.
        RuleGroupArn: string
        -- The descriptive name of the rule group. You can't change the name of a rule group after you create it.
        RuleGroupName: string
        -- The unique identifier for the rule group.
        RuleGroupId: string
        -- A description of the rule group.
        Description: string
        -- Indicates the type of the rule group. Valid values are:
        -- STATELESS - Stateless rules, for use in a Classic Network Firewall policy.
        -- STATEFUL - Stateful rules, for use in a Classic Network Firewall policy.
        -- FIREWALL - Firewall engine rules, for use in a Next Generation policy.
        -- IPS - IPS engine rules, for use in a Next Generation policy.
        Type: string
        -- The maximum operating resources that this rule group can use. Rule group capacity is fixed at creation.
        -- When you update a rule group, you are limited to this capacity. When you reference a rule group
        -- from a firewall policy, Network Firewall reserves this capacity for the rule group.
        -- You can retrieve the capacity that would be required for a rule group before you create the rule group by calling
        -- CreateRuleGroup with DryRun set to TRUE.
        Capacity: number
        -- Detailed information about the current status of a rule group.
        RuleGroupStatus: string
        -- The key:value pairs to associate with the resource.
        Tags: {Tag}
        -- The number of capacity units currently consumed by the rule group rules.
        ConsumedCapacity: number
        -- The number of firewall policies that use this rule group.
        NumberOfAssociations: number
        -- A complex type that contains the Amazon Web Services KMS encryption configuration settings for your rule group.
        EncryptionConfiguration: EncryptionConfiguration
        -- A complex type that contains metadata about the rule group that your own rule group is copied from. You can use the metadata to track the version updates made to the originating rule group.
        SourceMetadata: SourceMetadata
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the Amazon Simple Notification Service SNS topic that's
        -- used to record changes to the managed rule group. You can subscribe to the SNS topic to receive
        -- notifications when the managed rule group is modified, such as for new versions and for version
        -- expiration. For more information, see the Amazon Simple Notification Service Developer Guide..
        SnsTopic: string
        -- The last time that the rule group was changed.
        LastModifiedTime: number
        -- The list of analysis results for AnalyzeRuleGroup. If you set AnalyzeRuleGroup to TRUE in CreateRuleGroup, UpdateRuleGroup, or DescribeRuleGroup, Network Firewall analyzes the rule group and identifies the rules that might adversely effect your firewall's functionality. For example, if Network Firewall detects a rule that's routing traffic asymmetrically, which impacts the service's ability to properly process traffic, the service includes the rule in the list of analysis results.
        AnalysisResults: {AnalysisResult}
        -- A complex type containing the currently selected rule option fields that will be displayed for rule summarization returned by DescribeRuleGroupSummary.
        -- The RuleOptions specified in SummaryConfiguration
        -- Rule metadata organization preferences
        SummaryConfiguration: SummaryConfiguration
    end

    record CreateRuleGroupOutput
        -- A token used for optimistic locking. Network Firewall returns a token to your requests that access the rule group. The token marks the state of the rule group resource at the time of the request.
        -- To make changes to the rule group, you provide the token in your request. Network Firewall uses the token to ensure that the rule group hasn't changed since you last retrieved it. If it has changed, the operation fails with an InvalidTokenException. If this happens, retrieve the rule group again to get a current copy of it with a current token. Reapply your changes as needed, then try the operation again using the new token.
        UpdateToken: string
        -- The high-level properties of a rule group. This, along with the RuleGroup, define the rule group. You can retrieve all objects for a rule group by calling DescribeRuleGroup.
        RuleGroupResponse: RuleGroupResponse
    end

    enum DecryptionAction
        "DECRYPT"
        "BYPASS"
    end

    enum TLSInspectionScopeDeploymentMode
        "TRANSPARENT_FIREWALL_ONLY"
        "EXPLICIT_PROXY_ONLY"
        "ALL"
    end

    -- The domains that a ServerCertificateScope matches for SSL/TLS inspection on a proxy mode firewall.
    record DomainMatch
        -- The domains to match. You can specify an exact domain, such as example.com, or a domain with a single leading wildcard label, such as *.example.com. You can specify from 1 to 50 domains, and each domain can be up to 255 characters long.
        Domains: {string}
    end

    -- Settings that define the Secure Sockets Layer/Transport Layer Security (SSL/TLS) traffic that Network Firewall should decrypt for inspection by the stateful rule engine.
    record ServerCertificateScope
        -- The source IP addresses and address ranges to decrypt for inspection, in CIDR notation. If not specified, this
        -- matches with any source address.
        Sources: {Address}
        -- The destination IP addresses and address ranges to decrypt for inspection, in CIDR notation. If not specified, this
        -- matches with any destination address.
        Destinations: {Address}
        -- The source ports to decrypt for inspection, in Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) format. If not specified, this matches with any source port.
        -- You can specify individual ports, for example 1994, and you can specify port ranges, such as 1990:1994.
        SourcePorts: {PortRange}
        -- The destination ports to decrypt for inspection, in Transmission Control Protocol (TCP) format. If not specified, this matches with any destination port.
        -- You can specify individual ports, for example 1994, and you can specify port ranges, such as 1990:1994.
        DestinationPorts: {PortRange}
        -- The protocols to inspect for, specified using the assigned internet protocol number (IANA)
        -- for each protocol. If not specified, this matches with any protocol.
        -- Network Firewall currently supports only TCP.
        Protocols: {number}
        -- The domains that this scope matches, based on the host name in the client's HTTP CONNECT request. Domain matching applies only to proxy mode firewalls. If you specify DomainMatch, you must set DeploymentMode to EXPLICIT_PROXY_ONLY. If you don't specify DomainMatch, the scope matches traffic by its source, destination, ports, and protocols only.
        DomainMatch: DomainMatch
        -- Whether the firewall decrypts traffic that matches this scope. You can choose from the following values:
        -- DECRYPT - The firewall decrypts the traffic for inspection.
        -- BYPASS - The firewall doesn't decrypt the traffic.
        DecryptionAction: string
        -- The type of firewall that this scope applies to. If you don't specify a value, the scope applies to both types, the same as ALL. You can choose from the following values:
        -- ALL - The scope applies to both transparent firewalls and proxy mode firewalls.
        -- TRANSPARENT_FIREWALL_ONLY - The scope applies only to transparent firewalls.
        -- EXPLICIT_PROXY_ONLY - The scope applies only to proxy mode firewalls. A scope that specifies DomainMatch must use this value.
        DeploymentMode: string
    end

    -- Any Certificate Manager (ACM) Secure Sockets Layer/Transport Layer Security (SSL/TLS) server certificate that's associated with a ServerCertificateConfiguration. Used in a TLSInspectionConfiguration for inspection of inbound traffic to your firewall. You must request or import a SSL/TLS certificate into ACM for each domain Network Firewall needs to decrypt and inspect. Network Firewall uses the SSL/TLS certificates to decrypt specified inbound SSL/TLS traffic going to your firewall. For information about working with certificates in Certificate Manager, see Request a public certificate or Importing certificates in the Certificate Manager User Guide.
    record ServerCertificate
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the Certificate Manager SSL/TLS server certificate that's used for inbound SSL/TLS inspection.
        ResourceArn: string
    end

    -- Configures the Certificate Manager certificates and scope that Network Firewall uses to decrypt and re-encrypt traffic using a TLSInspectionConfiguration. You can configure ServerCertificates for inbound SSL/TLS inspection, a CertificateAuthorityArn for outbound SSL/TLS inspection, or both. For information about working with certificates for TLS inspection, see Using SSL/TLS server certficiates with TLS inspection configurations in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
    -- If a server certificate that's associated with your TLSInspectionConfiguration is revoked, deleted, or expired it can result in client-side TLS errors.
    record ServerCertificateConfiguration
        -- The list of server certificates to use for inbound SSL/TLS inspection.
        ServerCertificates: {ServerCertificate}
        -- A list of scopes.
        Scopes: {ServerCertificateScope}
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the imported certificate authority (CA) certificate within Certificate Manager (ACM) to use for outbound SSL/TLS inspection.
        -- The following limitations apply:
        -- You can use CA certificates that you imported into ACM, but you can't generate CA certificates with ACM.
        -- You can't use certificates issued by Private Certificate Authority.
        -- For more information about configuring certificates for outbound inspection, see Using SSL/TLS certificates with TLS inspection configurations in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        -- For information about working with certificates in ACM, see Importing certificates in the Certificate Manager User Guide.
        CertificateAuthorityArn: string
        -- When enabled, Network Firewall checks if the server certificate presented by the server in the SSL/TLS connection has a revoked or unkown status. If the certificate has an unknown or revoked status, you must specify the actions that Network Firewall takes on outbound traffic. To check the certificate revocation status, you must also specify a CertificateAuthorityArn in ServerCertificateConfiguration.
        CheckCertificateRevocationStatus: CheckCertificateRevocationStatusActions
        -- What the firewall does with SSL/TLS traffic that doesn't match any scope in this server certificate configuration. This setting applies only to proxy mode firewalls. If you don't specify a value, the firewall uses BYPASS. You can choose from the following values:
        -- BYPASS - The firewall doesn't decrypt the traffic.
        -- DECRYPT - The firewall decrypts the traffic for inspection. You can use DECRYPT only if every scope in the configuration sets DeploymentMode to EXPLICIT_PROXY_ONLY.
        DefaultDecryptionBehavior: string
    end

    -- The object that defines a TLS inspection configuration. This, along with TLSInspectionConfigurationResponse, define the TLS inspection configuration. You can retrieve all objects for a TLS inspection configuration by calling DescribeTLSInspectionConfiguration.
    -- Network Firewall uses a TLS inspection configuration to decrypt traffic. Network Firewall re-encrypts the traffic before sending it to its destination.
    -- To use a TLS inspection configuration, you add it to a new Network Firewall firewall policy, then you apply the firewall policy to a firewall. Network Firewall acts as a proxy service to decrypt and inspect the traffic traveling through your firewalls. You can reference a TLS inspection configuration from more than one firewall policy, and you can use a firewall policy in more than one firewall. For more information about using TLS inspection configurations, see
    -- Inspecting SSL/TLS traffic with TLS
    -- inspection configurations in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
    record TLSInspectionConfiguration
        -- Lists the server certificate configurations that are associated with the TLS configuration.
        ServerCertificateConfigurations: {ServerCertificateConfiguration}
    end

    record CreateTLSInspectionConfigurationInput
        -- The descriptive name of the TLS inspection configuration. You can't change the name of a TLS inspection configuration after you create it.
        TLSInspectionConfigurationName: string
        -- The object that defines a TLS inspection configuration. This, along with TLSInspectionConfigurationResponse, define the TLS inspection configuration. You can retrieve all objects for a TLS inspection configuration by calling DescribeTLSInspectionConfiguration.
        -- Network Firewall uses a TLS inspection configuration to decrypt traffic. Network Firewall re-encrypts the traffic before sending it to its destination.
        -- To use a TLS inspection configuration, you add it to a new Network Firewall firewall policy, then you apply the firewall policy to a firewall. Network Firewall acts as a proxy service to decrypt and inspect the traffic traveling through your firewalls. You can reference a TLS inspection configuration from more than one firewall policy, and you can use a firewall policy in more than one firewall. For more information about using TLS inspection configurations, see
        -- Inspecting SSL/TLS traffic with TLS
        -- inspection configurations in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
        TLSInspectionConfiguration: TLSInspectionConfiguration
        -- A description of the TLS inspection configuration.
        Description: string
        -- The key:value pairs to associate with the resource.
        Tags: {Tag}
        EncryptionConfiguration: EncryptionConfiguration
    end

    -- The high-level properties of a TLS inspection configuration. This, along with the TLSInspectionConfiguration, define the TLS inspection configuration. You can retrieve all objects for a TLS inspection configuration by calling DescribeTLSInspectionConfiguration.
    record TLSInspectionConfigurationResponse
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the TLS inspection configuration.
        TLSInspectionConfigurationArn: string
        -- The descriptive name of the TLS inspection configuration. You can't change the name of a TLS inspection configuration after you create it.
        TLSInspectionConfigurationName: string
        -- A unique identifier for the TLS inspection configuration. This ID is returned in the responses to create and list commands. You provide it to operations such as update and delete.
        TLSInspectionConfigurationId: string
        -- Detailed information about the current status of a TLSInspectionConfiguration. You can retrieve this for a TLS inspection configuration by calling DescribeTLSInspectionConfiguration and providing the TLS inspection configuration name and ARN.
        TLSInspectionConfigurationStatus: string
        -- A description of the TLS inspection configuration.
        Description: string
        -- The key:value pairs to associate with the resource.
        Tags: {Tag}
        -- The last time that the TLS inspection configuration was changed.
        LastModifiedTime: number
        -- The number of firewall policies that use this TLS inspection configuration.
        NumberOfAssociations: number
        -- A complex type that contains the Amazon Web Services KMS encryption configuration settings for your TLS inspection configuration.
        EncryptionConfiguration: EncryptionConfiguration
        -- A list of the certificates associated with the TLS inspection configuration.
        Certificates: {TlsCertificateData}
        CertificateAuthority: TlsCertificateData
    end

    record CreateTLSInspectionConfigurationOutput
        -- A token used for optimistic locking. Network Firewall returns a token to your requests that access the TLS inspection configuration. The token marks the state of the TLS inspection configuration resource at the time of the request.
        -- To make changes to the TLS inspection configuration, you provide the token in your request. Network Firewall uses the token to ensure that the TLS inspection configuration hasn't changed since you last retrieved it. If it has changed, the operation fails with an InvalidTokenException. If this happens, retrieve the TLS inspection configuration again to get a current copy of it with a current token. Reapply your changes as needed, then try the operation again using the new token.
        UpdateToken: string
        -- The high-level properties of a TLS inspection configuration. This, along with the TLSInspectionConfiguration, define the TLS inspection configuration. You can retrieve all objects for a TLS inspection configuration by calling DescribeTLSInspectionConfiguration.
        TLSInspectionConfigurationResponse: TLSInspectionConfigurationResponse
    end

    record CreateVpcEndpointAssociationInput
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the firewall.
        FirewallArn: string
        -- The unique identifier of the VPC where you want to create a firewall endpoint.
        VpcId: string
        SubnetMapping: SubnetMapping
        -- A description of the VPC endpoint association.
        Description: string
        -- The key:value pairs to associate with the resource.
        Tags: {Tag}
    end

    -- A VPC endpoint association defines a single subnet to use for a firewall endpoint for a Firewall.
    -- You can define VPC endpoint associations only in the Availability Zones that already have
    -- a subnet mapping defined in the Firewall resource.
    -- You can retrieve the list of Availability Zones that are available for use by calling DescribeFirewallMetadata.
    -- To manage firewall endpoints, first, in the Firewall specification, you specify a single VPC and one subnet
    -- for each of the Availability Zones where you want to use the firewall. Then you can define additional endpoints as
    -- VPC endpoint associations.
    -- You can use VPC endpoint associations to expand the protections of the firewall as follows:
    -- Protect multiple VPCs with a single firewall - You can use the firewall to protect other VPCs, either in your account or in accounts where the firewall is shared. You can only specify Availability Zones that already have a firewall endpoint defined in the Firewall subnet mappings.
    -- Define multiple firewall endpoints for a VPC in an Availability Zone - You can create additional firewall endpoints for the VPC that you have defined in the firewall, in any Availability Zone that already has an endpoint defined in the Firewall subnet mappings. You can create multiple VPC endpoint associations for any other VPC where you use the firewall.
    -- You can use Resource Access Manager to share a Firewall that you own with other accounts, which gives them the ability to use the firewall
    -- to create VPC endpoint associations. For information about sharing a firewall, see PutResourcePolicy
    -- in this guide and see
    -- Sharing Network Firewall resources in the Network Firewall Developer Guide.
    -- The status of the VPC endpoint association, which indicates whether it's ready to filter network traffic,
    -- is provided in the corresponding VpcEndpointAssociationStatus. You can retrieve both
    -- the association and its status by calling DescribeVpcEndpointAssociation.
    record VpcEndpointAssociation
        -- The unique identifier of the VPC endpoint association.
        VpcEndpointAssociationId: string
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of a VPC endpoint association.
        VpcEndpointAssociationArn: string
        -- The Amazon Resource Name (ARN) of the firewall.
        FirewallArn: string
        -- The unique identifier of the VPC for the endpoint association.
        VpcId: string
        SubnetMapping: SubnetMapping
        -- A description of the VPC endpoint association.
        Description: string
        -- The key:value pairs to associate with the resource.
        Tags: {Tag}
    end

    -- Detailed information about the current status of a VpcEndpointAssociation. You can retrieve this
    -- by calling DescribeVpcEndpointAssociation and providing the VPC endpoint association ARN.
